From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 08:15:29 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 32/33] perf: compiled VU1 programs keep registers in locals
 and work out flags only when read

vurecomp now holds the VF registers and ACC a program uses in locals and writes them back where it leaves, as it already did for VI. In VU memory every instruction stored and reloaded them, and a lane written alone and then read with the others stalled the load on the store.

In programs that never read a sticky status bit (all but the four that clear them with FSSET first), an op whose flags are live no longer computes them: FlagPipe::set_lazy keeps the result and the lanes, and mac(), status() and FSSET work the flags out from them when read. fl.commit brings r.mac and r.status up to date on the way out. Their sticky bits then miss the ops in between, which no UN5 program can see. efu takes VF[fs] by value so it reads the local.

UN5 battle replay on the Mac: the three hottest programs from 1.58/1.28/1.64 to 0.99/0.93/0.98 us per run. Frames are byte-identical. PS2_VU_CHECK still differs from the interpreter only in flags: MAC as before, and status only in sticky bits (0 differences in bits 0-5, battle and boot). Its report now prints which flag bits differ.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
